# Intent: The thinking cue (decision 23, overriding "tone off"): three animated dots on every face plus a quiet tone, on by default; spoken fillers stay optional and off, so Spanish needs no filler phrases. maipai_face_think_core.h is plain C: the dot wave (a left to right brightness wave over 1.2 s), the begin and end of a cue, a 45 s limit so a cue the host never ended stops by itself, and the tone rule (only when thinking_tone is on, never twice within 8 s). The cue starts on the thinking emotion (which draws the doubtful face) or the new tool self.face.thinking, and ends on any other emotion, any status change (listening, speaking, standby) or the limit. The dots always show; the tone is the shipped vibration sound played through maipai_sounds_play (new, a thin wrapper over the sound policy), so the policy, quiet hours and the hard mute may silence it. The dots and timer exist only while thinking. Behind CONFIG_MAIPAI_FACE_THINKING (default y); off, no dots or tone are compiled and the thinking emotion draws the doubtful face.
# Placement: unit (the cue is drawn and sounded on the robot; the cue code path stays in the core)
# Upstream: firmware/main/hal/board/stackchan_display.cc and hal/maipai_sounds.{h,cc} of the StackChan tree (MIT); new file hal/maipai_face_think_core.h
# Check after a flash: self.face.thinking {"on":true}: three dots pulse above the eyes and one quiet tone plays (none while muted); {"on":false} or a spoken reply removes them; with thinking_tone false the dots show and no tone plays; the dots end by themselves after 45 s.
